38/38d0f24ab003913a30051d64dda233980173939825d9cc6f34473b1cbdcdbeed.png)\n\n \n\nSo, the missing fraction model is:\n\n![](images/ac/ac8618a503eb2cfd41a7fa8851cdb0dbb7152325100ec922b533d9da6e63b7c5.png)" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073418 1071 "![](images/5a/5aef67e5f151465f09eba64456f4566cc984c97857d8e6203cd25d60952e183a.png)\n\n \n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5}.$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073418 1071 "![](images/5a/5aef67e5f151465f09eba64456f4566cc984c97857d8e6203cd25d60952e183a.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5}$." 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186073418 1074 "Since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{5}\n$$\n\nby $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe41721739d82df825d2a7e28bc19590fbc784354a58aa74137aceda0e6be202.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 3\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore,\n\n$$\n3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186073418 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{5}$ by $\\color{blue}3$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/fe/fe41721739d82df825d2a7e28bc19590fbc784354a58aa74137aceda0e6be202.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}3} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 3$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n3 \\times \\dfrac{1}{5} = \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073419 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/ee/eef3cec4311b03e7f4be59e367cbd3d6947afd73b16a00b487f28ae7ea2bf8b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 4 = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can swap the factors on the left-hand side. So, the following would be a correct answer too:\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186073419 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{1}{9}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{4}{9}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/ee/eef3cec4311b03e7f4be59e367cbd3d6947afd73b16a00b487f28ae7ea2bf8b8.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{9} \\times 4 = \\dfrac{4}{9}.\n$$\n\n**Note:** We can swap the factors on the left-hand side. So, the following would be a correct answer too:\n\n$$\n4 \\times \\dfrac{1}{9} = \\dfrac{4}{9}\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073420 1074 "We have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5}\n$$\n\non the left and\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\non the right.\n\n![](images/c1/c1efa667a938907fcdd5ba9d0172b37a7efb0a7b312e3025556c4444814d2ab3.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}$.\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{3}{5}\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}3}$." 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186073420 1074 "We have $\\dfrac{1}{5}$ on the left and $\\dfrac{3}{5}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/c1/c1efa667a938907fcdd5ba9d0172b37a7efb0a7b312e3025556c4444814d2ab3.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of ${\\color{blue}3}.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{5} \\times {\\color{blue}3} = \\dfrac{3}{5}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is ${\\color{blue}3}.$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425255 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425260 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425265 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425267 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073795 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425269 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425258 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425262 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425266 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073796 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425268 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425259 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425261 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425264 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073797 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425270 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425256 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425257 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425263 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073798 1038 #edb/ref 17592186425271 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186073982 1050 420.0 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074006 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074006 1071 "![](images/60/607bb957bbf20006ed9730d94f4a42a724d88ee64a6ab9f01b952c9e2cebe19e.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3 \\cdot \\frac{1}{2}$." 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186074006 1071 "![](images/60/607bb957bbf20006ed9730d94f4a42a724d88ee64a6ab9f01b952c9e2cebe19e.png)\n\nUse the model above to calculate $3\\times \\frac{1}{2}.$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074006 1074 "Since we need to multiply $\\frac{1}{2}$ by $3$, we repeat the fraction model $3$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/c1/c16178420d0d3a12c4640eae6ca4a3789087477bef276ba8e25af73096e844a9.png)\n\n \n\nThere are $1\\times 3=3$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, \n\n$$\n3\\times \\frac{1}{2}=\\frac{3}{2}.\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074408 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074408 1074 "We have $\\frac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\frac{3}{4}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/99/9992a38b2966e0f3cfd28c32c269c8acc0db00e443cf57783545fdf38f5f7ef9.png)\n\nThe shape on the left has $1$ shaded piece, and the shape on the right has $3$ shaded pieces. So the number of shaded pieces has increased by a factor of $3.$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$\n\nSo, the missing number is $3.$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074416 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186074416 1074 "We have $\\frac{1}{4}$ on the left and $\\frac{3}{4}$ on the right.\n\n![](images/3d/3dbb41b019fb0f25fe9dd2808071d31d1376bf571d4923646bd2831c47c264cb.png)\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\frac{1}{4}\\times 3=\\frac{3}{4}.\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186075016 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nFor example, let's consider the following product:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{3}\n$$\n\nusing a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}\n$$\n\nby ${\\color{blue}2}$, we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\nThere are\n\n$$\n{\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2\n$$\n\nshaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186075016 1064 "We can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers. \n\nFor example, let's consider the following product: \n\n$$\n\\dfrac 13 \\times 2\n$$\n\nFirst, we represent $\\dfrac{1}{3}$ using a model:\n\n![](images/f0/f08054646558ab944940f1f8f0d8a1077748f9882039e775c6cbdd4d307db102.png)\n\n \n\nMultiplication by a whole number is simply a repeated addition. And, since we need to multiply $\\dfrac{\\color{red}1}{3}$ by ${\\color{blue}2},$ we repeat the fraction model $\\color{blue}2$ times, as shown below.\n\n![](images/31/31fcef6d631262e49fc9c9297fd006f935fd7ee8182b0074d77ae22b626efbbc.png)\n\n \n\nThere are ${\\color{blue}2} \\times {\\color{red}1} = 2$ shaded pieces in total. Therefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n2 \\times \\dfrac{1}{3} = \\dfrac{2}{3}.\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186075017 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\n$$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7$.\n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have\n\n$$\n1 \\times 7 = 7\n$$\n\nshaded pieces:\n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 false] [17592186075017 1064 "There's another way we can use fraction models to multiply unit fractions by whole numbers.\n\nTo demonstrate, let's consider the multiplication problem below:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10} \\times 7\n$$\n\nWe start by drawing a fraction model to represent $\\dfrac{1}{10}.$\n\n![](images/62/62b50e658ed9bc691a9559548d09f87fbd3b33e007e81b5e78a3235ac4bc3280.png)\n\n \n\nNow, rather than repeating our model $7$ times (which would take a long time), we can instead multiply the number of shaded pieces by $7.$ \n\nOur model has $1$ shaded piece. So, the resulting model must have $1 \\times 7 = 7$ shaded pieces: \n\n![](images/2a/2a7d54b1d7a6a6013ee64b601d9dc0ba31c8a25dae0f5f69abba4b0fed0e8ee2.png)\n\nTherefore, we conclude that\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{1}{10}\\times 7 = \\dfrac{7}{10}.\n$$" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425158 1080 "9" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425158 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425158 1083 #uuid "011d959c-931e-57ed-8699-e64bfa41c4f0" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425159 1080 "images/a7/a75a15f2fdb23a006209d0915fc85f1e52ffd1f41df01ce74566712d3df6e038.png" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425159 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425159 1083 #uuid "0366f8ab-63b8-5b87-821a-791539b58141" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425160 1080 "8" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425160 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425160 1083 #uuid "046d5ee1-bdcd-5df6-b759-1fcc3a6000c5" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425161 1080 "images/22/225d9811e413462b180aa41c32448e6c10c9bd069388882c2b1df337fb08c738.png" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425161 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045497 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425161 1083 #uuid "06b0dd86-458a-5270-996b-bfb4e5cb9ac1" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425162 1080 "\\frac{1}{8}\\times 4=\\frac{4}{8}" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425162 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425162 1083 #uuid "08ba5908-dc8f-5c91-8920-1b09ad4b8ef2" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425163 1080 "\\frac{6}{12}" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425163 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425163 1083 #uuid "102302fd-38e4-568b-9f88-055c7f3d0c34" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425164 1080 "8" 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425164 1082 #edb/ref 17592186045498 13194139534549 17592186045417 true] [17592186425164 1